      "inputTokens": 127,
      "outputTokens": 876,
      "costUsd": 0.0079475,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here. Two, four, eight, sixteen. The gaps aren't equal, so this isn't arithmetic, but every term is double the one before. Here's what doubling can do. A sheet of paper is about a tenth of a millimetre thick. Fold it in half, and it's two layers thick. Fold again, and it's four. If you could fold it forty-two times, how thick would it be? A few centimetres? A metre? It would be thicker than the distance to the Moon. How can doubling turn something so thin into something so huge?"
    },
