      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 149,
      "outputTokens": 1161,
      "costUsd": 0.0105235,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's why doubling grows so fast. Plot the terms of three, six, twelve, and so on. An arithmetic sequence adds the same amount each time, so its points lie on a straight line. Here each step multiplies, so each jump is bigger than the last, and the points curve upward. The formula is three times two to the n minus one. The variable n is in the power, so a sub n is an exponential function of n. With a positive r, the points sit on an exponential curve. With a positive first term and r between zero and one, the points fall towards zero instead. When r is negative, the signs alternate, so the points jump from one side of the axis to the other."
    },
